  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1245 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nClearing skies this morning will pave the way for another warm\nday with highs mostly in the upper 80s to mid 90s this\nafternoon. Ridging remains in place over the Central Plains with\na strong low-level jet advecting in warm-moist air. This is \nleading to an axis of moisture oriented up the Missouri River \nacross eastern Nebraska and western Iowa. Subsidence from \nridging aloft and strong winds will help to mix down warmer \ntemperatures leading to a net drying effect in the sounding \nprofile. This should inhibit shower and storm development this \nafternoon. We're watching the front currently in Wyoming for it \nto cross north into the Dakotas and drop south into NE Nebraska \ntonight, triggering shower and storm chances for NE Nebraska \novernight into Tuesday morning. While we do have a Marginal Risk\n(level 1 of 5) for severe storms, it looks like the better \nchance for stronger storms should stay north in SE South Dakota.\nEven that isn't looking as good as it did in earlier runs of \nthe CAMs. Still, that's where the better shear is found. Best \nwindow for our area is probably midnight-2am, with some \npotential as early as 9pm. Greatest hazards will be damaging \nwinds and large hail. \n\nTuesday we'll have the frontal boundary, stalled across\nnortheast Nebraska through the morning. Looking at the upper-\nlevel flow, the front appears to stall along the dampened ridge,\nflattened by a series of troughs ejecting out of Washington and\nBritish Columbia. The second trough finally kicks the crest of\nthe ridge east, allowing the front to drop south across the \nrest of our area Tuesday afternoon and evening. With this, \nexpect a line of showers and storms to develop along the I-80 \ncorridor and shift southeast across the rest of southeast \nNebraska and southwest Iowa Tuesday evening and overnight. While\nwe will be plenty unstable, shear is limited along and ahead of\nthe front making for only another Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) \nfor severe storms.\n\nWe're also monitoring the heat tomorrow along and south of the\nstalled frontal boundary. With moisture pooling along the\nfrontal boundary and temperatures forecast to reach into the \nmid-to-upper 90s, heat indices are forecast to hit 103-107\nacross much of southeast Nebraska and southwest Iowa. Lower\nconfidence right along the front from Lincoln through Omaha\neastward as shower and storm development may be occurring right\nas we hit peak heating. This leads to a very short window of\nopportunity for heat indices to hit Heat Advisory criteria of \n105, while areas south have a better chance. Have issued a Heat\nAdvisory for areas with the best chance of hitting this\ncriteria. \n\nShowers and storms will linger across far southeast Nebraska and\nsouthwest Iowa into Wednesday morning, mostly clearing out by\nthe afternoon. Temperatures will be milder behind the front with\nhighs near normal for this time of year, in the upper 70s to low\n80s. We stay dry and mild through Thursday as zonal flow\nmaintains across the region aloft and a cooler Canadian high \nsits in place near the surface. Our next chance for showers and\nstorms looks to be Friday when a trough brings another front\nthrough the region. The warm front lifting north in the morning\nwill bring temperatures back up into the mid-to-upper 80s \nFriday afternoon with showers and storms expected to develop \nalong and ahead of the cold front Friday afternoon and evening. \n\nBehind this next system, expect another Canadian High to\nmaintain seasonably mild temperatures with highs in the upper\n70s to low 80s into the weekend. More rain expected Sunday into\nMonday with broad troughing in place aloft helping to reinforce\nnorthwesterly flow and CAA across our area.",
